On Fri, 2010-04-23, Patric Mueller wrote:
> Jorgen Grahn <grahn+nntp@snipabacken.se> wrote:
>>
>> Citation needed -- or are you in the original Dev Team?  I doubt they
>> had a stack space problem on their original target architectures,
>> which IIRC didn't include the home computers.

Someone corrected me upthread -- Atari and MS-DOS were supported by
the first Nethack.  I assumed it had all started with VMS and Unix.

>> I think I had to give
>> Nethack extra stack on my Amiga,
>
> That's more a deficiency of the fixed and default low stack size on
> the Amiga.

Depends on how you see it, doesn't it?  Either it's a limitation you
have to live with (my attitude as an Amiga programmer) or it's a
design fault. For a multitasking OS living in 512 KB with no virtual
memory, it was probably a sane choice.  Embedded OSes like VxWorks and
OSE still do it like that.

> But was this also necessary for the early port? I know that the 3.4.x
> port version was memory and stack hungry.

I stopped running Nethack on my Amiga in 1998 or so when I switched to
Linux, so my vague memory of increasing the stack size was for older
versions.

>> which was of course a fairly late port.
>
> Depends on how you define "late". NetHack 1.3d, 1.4f and 2.2a were all
> released 1987, version 2.3e was released in April 1988. 
>
> The Amiga-fied port of NetHack 2.3e by Olaf Seibert was released 2 Dec
> 1988 on comp.sources.amiga and 10 Mar 1989 on Fish Disk 189, a few
> months before 3.0.0 came out which already had Olaf as official Amiga
> port maintainer.

I stand corrected again. I assumed at the time that Nethack was fairly
old on Unix/VMS -- not including Hack and rogue.  By the way, I think
my first Nethack version was 2.3e, even though this was in 1992 or so,
years after 3.0.0 came out according to you. It was easier to miss new
releases before the WWW ...

